// Question pool 15: operations, governance, migration and the edge. Exam level. Data only; ids key saved stats, never rename or reuse.
window.QUIZ.push(
{id:"q15-001", day:15, dom:2, tags:["Infrastructure as code"],
 q:"A company manages its production environment with one AWS CloudFormation stack that includes an Amazon RDS DB instance, an Auto Scaling group and several security groups. An engineer has edited the template to change the DB instance class and the DB instance identifier. Before anything is applied, the release manager wants to see exactly which resources would be added, modified or replaced, so that an unexpected replacement of the database can be stopped. Which approach meets this requirement with the LEAST risk?",
 o:["Run drift detection on the stack and review the resources that are reported as drifted.",
    "Create a change set from the edited template, review it, and execute it only if it is acceptable.",
    "Update the stack directly and rely on automatic rollback if the database is replaced.",
    "Enable termination protection on the stack and then update the stack with the edited template."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Drift detection compares the stack's deployed resources with the template that is already in use. It reports changes made outside CloudFormation and says nothing about what an edited template would do.",
    "Correct. A change set previews how proposed changes affect running resources, including which ones CloudFormation will add, modify, delete or replace. Nothing changes until someone executes the change set, so a replacement of the database can be seen and stopped.",
    "A direct update starts making changes straight away. Rollback happens when an update fails, and a successful replacement of the database is not a failure, so the old instance would already be gone.",
    "Termination protection applies only to attempts to delete the stack. It does not preview an update or stop an update from replacing a resource."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudformation"},

{id:"q15-002", day:15, dom:2, tags:["Infrastructure as code"],
 q:"A data team launches a temporary analytics environment from an AWS CloudFormation template each quarter. The stack contains an Amazon Redshift cluster and several Amazon EBS volumes. When the quarter ends, the whole stack is deleted. The company wants the data on the cluster and the volumes to be recoverable afterwards, but it does not want the cluster or the volumes to keep running and generating compute or volume charges. Which change to the template meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add <code>DeletionPolicy: Retain</code> to the cluster and the volumes.",
    "Add <code>UpdateReplacePolicy: Snapshot</code> to the cluster and the volumes.",
    "Add <code>DeletionPolicy: Snapshot</code> to the cluster and the volumes.",
    "Enable termination protection on the stack when it is created."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Retain keeps the resources themselves when the stack is deleted. The cluster and volumes would keep existing and keep incurring their normal charges, which is what the company wants to avoid.",
    "UpdateReplacePolicy acts only when a stack update replaces a resource with a new physical resource. It does nothing when the stack is deleted.",
    "Correct. For resources that support snapshots, such as AWS::Redshift::Cluster and AWS::EC2::Volume, a Snapshot deletion policy makes CloudFormation create a snapshot before it deletes the resource. Only the snapshots remain, and they can be restored later.",
    "Termination protection makes every attempt to delete the stack fail. The environment could then not be removed at all until someone turns the protection off, and no snapshot is taken."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudformation"},

{id:"q15-003", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Infrastructure as code"],
 q:"A security review found that an engineer opened an extra inbound port on a security group directly in the Amazon EC2 console during an incident. The security group and about 60 other resources belong to an AWS CloudFormation stack whose template has not changed for months. The team wants to find every resource in the stack whose current configuration no longer matches the template, and to see which property values differ, with the least effort. What should the team do?",
 o:["Search AWS CloudTrail event history for every API call made against each of the stack's resources.",
    "Update the stack again with the same unchanged template and compare the stack events.",
    "Enable termination protection on the stack and review the stack's resource list.",
    "Run drift detection on the stack and review the drift results for each resource."],
 a:[3],
 x:["CloudTrail records who called which API, so it can show who made a change. The team would still have to look up dozens of resources one by one and work out for itself how each differs from the template.",
    "A stack update acts on the differences between what is submitted and the stack's current template and parameters. Submitting the same template again gives CloudFormation nothing to change and does not report changes made outside CloudFormation.",
    "Termination protection only blocks deletion of the stack. The resource list shows what the stack contains, not whether a resource was changed outside CloudFormation.",
    "Correct. Drift detection compares the expected property values from the template and its parameters with the actual values of each supported resource, marks the stack and each resource as drifted or in sync, and returns detailed information about every drifted resource."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudformation"},

{id:"q15-004", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Infrastructure as code"],
 q:"A holding company owns 14 AWS accounts that came from acquisitions. The accounts are standalone: they are not members of an organization in AWS Organizations, and legal constraints prevent joining them to one this year. The platform team must deploy the same baseline of IAM roles and logging resources to all 14 accounts in three Regions from one template, and roll out later template changes to every account and Region with a single operation. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["A CloudFormation stack set with self-managed permissions, with the required IAM roles created in the administrator account and in each target account.",
    "A CloudFormation stack set with service-managed permissions and automatic deployment turned on for the 14 accounts.",
    "One CloudFormation root stack in the platform account that contains a nested stack for each account and Region.",
    "One CloudFormation stack in the platform account whose outputs are exported and imported by a stack in each of the other accounts."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. StackSets creates, updates and deletes stacks across accounts and Regions with a single operation. With self-managed permissions the team creates the IAM roles that set up trust between the administrator account and each target account, and that model can deploy to any account in which the roles can be created.",
    "Service-managed permissions deploy to accounts that are managed by AWS Organizations, and automatic deployment applies to accounts added to the organization. These accounts are standalone, so this model is not available.",
    "A nested stack is created inside its parent stack, in the parent's account and Region. Nesting splits a template into reusable pieces; it does not deploy resources into other accounts or Regions.",
    "Exported outputs are available to other stacks in the same account and Region only, and they share values, not resources. Each account would still need its own stack deployed and updated separately."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudformation"},

{id:"q15-005", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Infrastructure as code","IAM roles & cross-account"],
 q:"A company wants its data scientists to launch approved analysis environments by themselves. Each environment is defined by a CloudFormation template owned by the platform team and contains Amazon EC2 instances, an Amazon RDS database and IAM roles. The data scientists must not be given permissions to create these resources directly, and they must not be able to launch anything other than the approved templates. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Publish each template as a product in an AWS Service Catalog portfolio and grant the data scientists access to the portfolio.",
    "Store the templates in an Amazon S3 bucket and allow the data scientists <code>cloudformation:CreateStack</code> on any template.",
    "Add a launch constraint to each product that names an IAM role for Service Catalog to assume when the product is launched.",
    "Attach the <code>PowerUserAccess</code> managed policy to the data scientists and ask them to use only the approved templates.",
    "Deploy the templates to the data scientists' account as a CloudFormation stack set with automatic deployment."],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["Correct. Service Catalog lets administrators put approved products into portfolios and grant access to them, so users can find and launch only those products.",
    "With their own credentials the data scientists would need permissions for every service the templates use, and <code>CreateStack</code> on any template lets them launch templates nobody approved.",
    "Correct. Without a launch constraint, users launch products with their own IAM credentials and need permissions for CloudFormation and the underlying services. With a launch role, Service Catalog provisions the resources, so the users' own permissions can stay at the minimum.",
    "This grants broad permissions to create resources directly, which the requirement rules out, and a request to use only approved templates is not a control.",
    "A stack set would create the environments centrally for everyone at once. It gives the data scientists no self-service way to launch an approved environment when they need one."],
 ref:[], doc:"service-catalog"},

{id:"q15-006", day:15, dom:2, tags:["Infrastructure as code"],
 q:"A network team owns a CloudFormation stack that creates a VPC, its subnets and shared security groups in one account and Region. About 20 application stacks in the same account and Region, each owned by a different team and updated on its own schedule, must use those subnet and security group IDs. The teams must not hard-code the IDs, and the network team must be prevented from deleting a subnet's stack output while an application stack still depends on it. Which approach meets these requirements?",
 o:["Turn every application stack into a nested stack of the network stack and pass the IDs down as parameters.",
    "Write the IDs to tags on the VPC and have each application team copy them into its stack parameters.",
    "Convert the network stack into a stack set and add each application stack as a stack instance.",
    "Export the IDs as outputs of the network stack and reference them with <code>Fn::ImportValue</code> in the application stacks."],
 a:[3],
 x:["With nested stacks all resources are deployed and managed from a single root stack. The 20 application stacks would lose their separate owners and schedules, and every change would go through the network team's stack.",
    "Copying IDs by hand into parameters is the hard-coding the teams want to avoid, and nothing stops the network team from removing a subnet that an application still uses.",
    "A stack set deploys one template as stacks in several accounts and Regions. It does not share values between different stacks in one account.",
    "Correct. A stack exports output values, and other stacks in the same account and Region import them with <code>Fn::ImportValue</code>. While another stack imports an output, the exporting stack cannot be deleted and the exported value cannot be changed."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudformation"},

{id:"q15-007", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Governance"],
 q:"A company governs 60 accounts with AWS Control Tower. Detective controls report noncompliant resources, such as Amazon RDS instances without storage encryption, hours after delivery pipelines have created them. All teams provision infrastructure through AWS CloudFormation. The compliance team wants the configuration of each resource to be checked during the stack operation, so that a noncompliant resource is never provisioned. What should a solutions architect enable on the organizational units?",
 o:["Additional detective controls, which evaluate resources with AWS Config rules in each account.",
    "Proactive controls, which evaluate resources with CloudFormation hooks.",
    "The Region deny control, which blocks actions outside the governed Regions.",
    "CloudFormation drift detection on every stack, run on a daily schedule."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Detective controls are implemented with AWS Config rules and detect noncompliance of resources that already exist in the accounts. That is the after-the-fact reporting the company already has.",
    "Correct. A proactive control scans resources before they are provisioned, and resources that are not compliant are not provisioned. Proactive controls are implemented with CloudFormation hooks and apply to resources deployed through CloudFormation, which is how every team here works.",
    "The Region deny control is a preventive control that disallows operations outside the Regions selected for the landing zone. It does not look at how a resource in an allowed Region is configured.",
    "Drift detection shows where a deployed resource no longer matches its own template. A template that asks for an unencrypted database is deployed exactly as written, so there is no drift to find."],
 ref:[], doc:"controltower"},

{id:"q15-008", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Governance"],
 q:"A company brings its own database licenses to AWS. Its agreement with the vendor allows the software to run on a total of 480 vCPUs. Teams in several accounts of the company's organization launch Amazon EC2 instances from a shared AMI that contains the software, and last year an audit found more vCPUs in use than were licensed. The company must track usage across the accounts and must stop new instances from launching when the licensed number of vCPUs is reached. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Create an AWS Config rule that marks instances launched from the AMI as noncompliant when the vCPU total is exceeded.",
    "Use AWS Systems Manager Inventory to list the installed software and review the totals every week.",
    "Create a self-managed license in AWS License Manager with a hard limit of 480 vCPUs and associate it with the AMI.",
    "Run the instances on Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts and review host utilization in AWS Cost Explorer."],
 a:[2],
 x:["A Config rule evaluates resources after they exist and reports noncompliance. The over-licensed instance would already be running, and the company would have to build the counting logic itself.",
    "An inventory of installed software shows what is running but enforces nothing, and a weekly manual review is the kind of process that already failed the audit.",
    "Correct. License Manager tracks software licensed by vCPUs, cores, sockets or machines across accounts in an organization. Licensing rules can be attached to an AMI, every launch from it is tracked automatically, and with a hard limit License Manager can prevent resources from launching.",
    "Dedicated Hosts give visibility of physical cores and sockets for licenses that are tied to hardware. They do not count vCPUs against an entitlement or block a launch, and Cost Explorer reports cost and usage only."],
 ref:[], doc:"license-manager"},

{id:"q15-009", day:15, dom:2, tags:["Governance"],
 q:"A company runs several hundred Amazon EC2 instances in one account. Twice this year AWS scheduled an instance for retirement because of degraded hardware, and the operations team found out only after the instance had stopped, because the notices went to an unmonitored mailbox. The team wants a message in its operations channel as soon as AWS reports a scheduled event that affects one of the account's own resources. The company has the Basic Support plan. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches AWS Health events and sends them to an Amazon SNS topic.",
    "Check the public AWS Health Dashboard service health page for the Region every morning.",
    "Run a Lambda function on a schedule that queries the AWS Health API for new events.",
    "Create a CloudWatch alarm on the <code>StatusCheckFailed_System</code> metric of every instance."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. AWS Health sends account-specific events, for example for EC2 instances that are scheduled for updates, and all customers can receive AWS Health events through EventBridge at no additional cost. A rule can forward them to SNS and from there to the team's channel.",
    "The public service health page shows only public events that are not specific to an account. A retirement that affects one of the company's own instances does not appear there.",
    "Programmatic access to the AWS Health API requires a Business Support+, Enterprise Support or Unified Operations plan. The company has Basic Support.",
    "This alarm reacts when a system status check has already failed. It gives no advance notice of an event that AWS has scheduled for a later date."],
 ref:[], doc:"health"},

{id:"q15-010", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Governance"],
 q:"A company runs 500 Amazon EC2 instances with Windows Server and Amazon Linux, plus 60 Linux servers in its own data center. A new security policy requires operating system patches to be installed on all of them every Saturday night, only from a list of patches that the security team has approved, and it requires a report of which servers are missing patches. The company wants one mechanism for all servers with the LEAST operational overhead. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable Amazon Inspector for the account and review its vulnerability findings every week.",
    "Send a patch script to all servers every Saturday with AWS Systems Manager Run Command.",
    "Bake a new AMI every week and replace all instances through instance refresh in their Auto Scaling groups.",
    "Use AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager with custom patch baselines and a scheduled patching operation for all managed nodes."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Amazon Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure and reports findings. It does not install patches, and it has no list of approved patches.",
    "Run Command can run a script at scale, but the company would have to write and maintain scripts for each operating system, schedule them, and build its own approval list and compliance report.",
    "Replacing instances from a new AMI works only for instances in Auto Scaling groups. It does nothing for the servers in the data center and gives no report of missing patches.",
    "Correct. Patch Manager automates patching of managed nodes, which can be EC2 instances, on-premises servers and virtual machines. Custom patch baselines define which patches are approved, patching can run on a recurring schedule, and after a scan it reports which nodes are out of compliance and which patches are missing."],
 ref:[], doc:"ssm"},

{id:"q15-011", day:15, dom:2, tags:["Governance"],
 q:"A company must move 300 Amazon EC2 instances in 12 accounts and three Regions to a newer instance family. For each instance the procedure is the same: create an AMI as a fallback, stop the instance, change the instance type, start it again and verify that it is running. A change manager must approve each batch before it starts, only a limited number of instances may be processed at the same time, and the rollout must stop automatically when too many instances fail. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST development effort?",
 o:["A shell script sent with AWS Systems Manager Run Command to all instances, with rate control and an error threshold.",
    "An AWS Systems Manager Automation runbook with an approval step, run with concurrency and error thresholds.",
    "A Systems Manager State Manager association that applies the new instance type on a schedule.",
    "A Patch Manager patch baseline that lists the new instance family, run in a maintenance window."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Run Command runs commands on the operating system of managed nodes through the agent. Stopping an instance and changing its type are actions on the instance from outside, and a stopped instance cannot run the rest of a script.",
    "Correct. Automation runbooks carry out multi-step maintenance tasks on AWS resources such as EC2 instances. An automation can require approvals, can run across multiple accounts and Regions from a central place, and lets the team set how many resources are targeted concurrently and how many errors stop it.",
    "State Manager keeps managed nodes in a defined state by applying an association repeatedly. This is a one-time, multi-step change with an approval gate, not a state to keep enforcing.",
    "Patch Manager installs operating system and application patches on managed nodes. A patch baseline defines approved patches and has nothing to do with instance types."],
 ref:[], doc:"ssm"},

{id:"q15-012", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Governance","Organizations & SCPs"],
 q:"A company with 45 accounts in AWS Organizations charges costs back by a <code>CostCenter</code> tag. Two problems keep appearing. Some teams enter values that are not on the finance team's list, and some teams launch Amazon EC2 instances with no <code>CostCenter</code> tag at all. Finance wants both problems blocked at the moment an instance is created, in every member account, including accounts added later. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Attach a tag policy that lists the allowed <code>CostCenter</code> values and enforces them for EC2 resource types.",
    "Activate <code>CostCenter</code> as a cost allocation tag in the management account.",
    "Deploy the AWS Config <code>required-tags</code> managed rule to all accounts.",
    "Attach an SCP that denies <code>ec2:RunInstances</code> when the request does not include a <code>CostCenter</code> tag.",
    "Use Tag Editor every week to find untagged instances and add the tag."],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Correct. A tag policy can define the allowed values for a tag key and, when enforcement is turned on for a resource type, prevents noncompliant tagging requests from completing. Attached at the root, it also applies to accounts that join later.",
    "Activating a cost allocation tag makes the tag available in billing reports. It blocks nothing.",
    "The rule reports resources that lack a tag after they have been created. It detects the problem instead of preventing the launch.",
    "Correct. Tag policy enforcement does not act on resources that are created without the tag: AWS states that it does not enforce missing tag keys and points to SCPs to stop principals from creating resources when the request lacks the specified tags.",
    "Tag Editor is a way to find and fix tags by hand afterwards. Nothing is blocked at creation, and the work repeats every week."],
 ref:[], doc:"tag-policies"},

{id:"q15-013", day:15, dom:3, tags:["Governance"],
 q:"A company runs 400 Amazon EC2 instances that were sized years ago by different teams. Some applications respond slowly at peak times and the teams suspect that their instances are too small, while other instances look far larger than needed. The architecture team wants, for each instance, a finding that says whether it is under-provisioned, over-provisioned or optimized, with recommended instance types based on recent utilization metrics. The team does not want to build its own analysis. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Use the AWS Trusted Advisor check for low utilization Amazon EC2 instances.",
    "Create AWS Budgets usage budgets for each team's instances with alerts.",
    "Review the Savings Plans purchase recommendations in AWS Cost Explorer.",
    "Opt in to AWS Compute Optimizer and review its EC2 instance recommendations."],
 a:[3],
 x:["This check flags instances whose daily CPU utilization and network I/O stayed very low, so it finds idle or nearly idle instances. It does not identify instances that are too small for their workload.",
    "A budget tracks cost or usage against a threshold and sends alerts. It does not analyse how well an instance type fits its workload.",
    "These recommendations suggest how much to commit to in order to pay less for existing usage. They do not say whether an instance is the right size.",
    "Correct. Compute Optimizer analyses the configuration and CloudWatch utilization metrics of resources and classifies each EC2 instance as under-provisioned, over-provisioned or optimized, with recommendations that aim to reduce cost and improve performance."],
 ref:[], doc:"compute-optimizer"},

{id:"q15-014", day:15, dom:3, tags:["CloudWatch","Containers"],
 q:"A platform team runs 30 microservices on Amazon EKS. It monitors them with self-managed Prometheus servers and a self-managed Grafana server on EC2 instances. As the clusters grew, the Prometheus servers ran out of memory several times and metrics were lost. The team wants to stop operating the monitoring servers but keep its existing PromQL alert rules and Grafana dashboards, and engineers should sign in to the dashboards through the company's IAM Identity Center. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Move the Prometheus and Grafana servers to larger instances in an Auto Scaling group across three Availability Zones.",
    "Turn on CloudWatch Container Insights for the clusters and rebuild the dashboards as CloudWatch dashboards.",
    "Send the metrics to Amazon Managed Service for Prometheus and move the dashboards to Amazon Managed Grafana.",
    "Stream the metrics to Amazon OpenSearch Service and rebuild the dashboards in OpenSearch Dashboards."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Larger instances in more Availability Zones may delay the next outage, but the team still installs, scales, patches and repairs every monitoring server itself.",
    "Container Insights is a managed way to collect container metrics and logs, but this option throws away the Grafana dashboards the team wants to keep and has them rebuilt in a different tool.",
    "Correct. Amazon Managed Service for Prometheus is a serverless, Prometheus-compatible service for container metrics that uses the same data model and PromQL and scales ingestion, storage and querying automatically. Amazon Managed Grafana runs Grafana workspaces without servers to manage, uses the Prometheus service as a data source and integrates with IAM Identity Center.",
    "OpenSearch Service is a search and log analytics service. The alert rules written in PromQL and the Grafana dashboards would both have to be rebuilt."],
 ref:[], doc:"prometheus"},

{id:"q15-015", day:15, dom:2, tags:["CloudWatch"],
 q:"An order-processing application has 24 Amazon CloudWatch alarms on its load balancer, instances, queue and database. Every alarm notifies the on-call engineer through the same Amazon SNS topic. During an outage last month the engineer received more than 40 pages within a few minutes for what was a single incident. The team wants the engineer to be paged once when the application has a problem, and it wants to keep all 24 alarms so that the cause can still be seen in the console. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create a composite alarm over the 24 alarms, give it the SNS action, and remove the actions from the 24 alarms.",
    "Raise the threshold and the number of evaluation periods of all 24 alarms so that each one triggers less often.",
    "Replace the 24 alarms with one alarm on a metric math expression that adds all of the monitored metrics together.",
    "Add a filter policy to the on-call subscription so that only the first message in each hour is delivered."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. A composite alarm determines its state from the states of other alarms and reduces alarm noise by taking action only at the aggregated level. When any underlying alarm goes into ALARM, the composite alarm notifies once; further alarms do not send new notifications, and the underlying alarms can be created without actions.",
    "Less sensitive alarms react later and can miss real problems, and several alarms can still fire together during one incident.",
    "Request counts, CPU percentages, queue depth and latency have different units and thresholds, so their sum means nothing, and the single alarm no longer shows which part failed.",
    "An SNS filter policy matches message attributes or message content. It has no notion of the first message in a time window, so it cannot remove duplicates."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudwatch"},

{id:"q15-016", day:15, dom:1, tags:["CloudWatch"],
 q:"A company runs one application across 25 accounts in AWS Organizations, all in the same Region. During incidents, engineers lose time switching roles between accounts to look at metrics, logs and traces. The operations team wants to search and visualize the metrics, log groups and traces of all 25 accounts from a single operations account without switching roles, and accounts created later in the organization must be included automatically. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Configure the operations account as a monitoring account for CloudWatch cross-account observability.",
    "Create an organization trail in AWS CloudTrail that delivers events to the operations account.",
    "Link the source accounts to the monitoring account through AWS Organizations.",
    "Create an AWS Config aggregator for the organization in the operations account.",
    "Create one IAM role per account and a saved role-switch link for each one in the console."],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["Correct. With CloudWatch cross-account observability a monitoring account can view and work with the metrics, log groups and traces of linked source accounts in a Region, without account boundaries.",
    "An organization trail collects records of API activity. It does not bring metrics, application logs or traces into one view.",
    "Correct. Source accounts can be linked through AWS Organizations, which AWS recommends because accounts created later in the organization are onboarded as source accounts automatically.",
    "A Config aggregator collects resource configuration and compliance data from accounts and Regions. It holds no metrics, logs or traces.",
    "This is the role switching that costs the engineers time today, and each new account would need its role and link set up by hand."],
 ref:[], doc:"cw-cross-account"},

{id:"q15-017", day:15, dom:2, tags:["Migration & transfer"],
 q:"A company must move 180 servers out of a data center before its lease ends in four months. The servers are a mix of physical machines and VMware virtual machines that run Windows Server and several Linux distributions. The applications will not be changed for now. Each application must be tested in AWS before its migration without disturbing the source server, and the final cutover for each application must fit into a short maintenance window. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Export each server as an image with VM Import/Export and launch EC2 instances from the images.",
    "Copy each server's file systems to Amazon EBS volumes with AWS DataSync and attach them to new instances.",
    "Replicate the servers with AWS Transform MGN, launch test instances, and then cut over each application.",
    "Rebuild each server on a new EC2 instance and move its databases with AWS Database Migration Service."],
 a:[2],
 x:["An exported image is a point-in-time copy. Each server would have to stay offline, or lose the changes made after the export, while its image is imported, and nothing keeps the copy in sync until a cutover.",
    "DataSync transfers file and object data between storage systems and services. It does not convert a running server with its operating system and boot volume into an EC2 instance.",
    "Correct. AWS Transform MGN (formerly AWS Application Migration Service, the name the exam guide uses) replicates physical, virtual and cloud servers continuously at block level, supports non-disruptive tests before cutover, and cuts over in windows that are typically minutes long.",
    "Rebuilding 180 servers by hand is a re-installation project, not a rehost, and is unlikely to fit into four months. DMS migrates databases, not whole servers."],
 ref:[], doc:"mgn"},

{id:"q15-018", day:15, dom:3, tags:["Migration & transfer"],
 q:"A retailer keeps its orders in an on-premises PostgreSQL database that stays in production. Analysts want to query order data in an Amazon S3 data lake with Amazon Athena. The existing rows must be copied once, and afterwards every insert, update and delete must reach the data lake continuously, in a columnar format, without custom code and without a nightly batch window. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["An AWS DataSync task that copies the PostgreSQL data directory to the S3 bucket every hour.",
    "A nightly <code>pg_dump</code> export uploaded to the S3 bucket by a scheduled script.",
    "An AWS Transfer Family SFTP server to which the database host uploads change files.",
    "An AWS DMS task with full load and change data capture that writes Apache Parquet files to the S3 bucket."],
 a:[3],
 x:["DataSync copies files and objects. The files in a database's data directory are the engine's internal storage, not rows that Athena can query, and copying them from a running database is not change capture.",
    "A nightly dump is the batch window the retailer wants to avoid, it is a script the retailer must maintain, and a plain SQL dump is not a columnar format.",
    "Transfer Family gives partners and applications a managed SFTP endpoint. Something would still have to extract the changes from the database and produce the files.",
    "Correct. AWS DMS can replicate from a supported source to Amazon S3 as a target with a full load followed by ongoing change data capture, which is a documented way to feed a data lake, and it can write the data in Apache Parquet format instead of the default CSV."],
 ref:[], doc:"dms"},

{id:"q15-019", day:15, dom:4, tags:["Migration & transfer"],
 q:"A company wants to stop paying for commercial database licenses by moving 12 Microsoft SQL Server databases to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible Edition. The databases contain thousands of stored procedures and functions. Before the budget is approved, the CTO wants to know for each database how much of the schema and code can be converted automatically and how much must be rewritten by hand. No data should be moved at this stage. Which approach gives this information with the LEAST effort?",
 o:["Create a migration project in AWS DMS Schema Conversion and generate an assessment report for each database.",
    "Run an AWS DMS full-load task from each database to a test Aurora cluster and compare the row counts.",
    "Rehost the SQL Server hosts on Amazon EC2 with AWS Transform MGN and measure their utilization.",
    "Restore native backups of the databases to Amazon RDS for SQL Server and run the applications' test suites."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. DMS Schema Conversion reads the source metadata and creates a conversion assessment report that shows what it can convert automatically and what needs manual work. AWS describes the report as the way to estimate the effort of a migration before committing to it, and no data is moved.",
    "A DMS data migration task moves data and creates only the tables and their primary keys on the target. It moves data, which the CTO does not want yet, and it says nothing about converting procedures and functions.",
    "Rehosting moves the servers unchanged, with the same engine and the same licenses. It gives no information about a conversion to PostgreSQL.",
    "Moving to RDS for SQL Server keeps the same engine and its license cost. Because nothing is converted, it cannot show how much conversion work there is."],
 ref:[], doc:"dms"},

{id:"q15-020", day:15, dom:4, tags:["Migration & transfer"],
 q:"After an acquisition, a company must bring 70 TB of documents from a bucket in another cloud provider's object storage into Amazon S3. The acquired teams keep writing to the old bucket for the next three months, so after the first copy, new and changed objects must be copied every night until the cutover. Transfers must be encrypted, and the data must be verified after it is copied. The company wants to avoid running its own transfer servers. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Launch a fleet of EC2 instances that run a scheduled copy script from the other cloud to S3.",
    "Create an AWS DataSync task from the other cloud's object storage to the S3 bucket and run it on a nightly schedule.",
    "Create an AWS Transfer Family SFTP server and ask the acquired teams to upload their documents to it.",
    "Deploy an Amazon S3 File Gateway and mount the other cloud's bucket on it as a file share."],
 a:[1],
 x:["This could work, but the company would operate the instances, write the incremental logic and build verification and retries itself, which is what it wants to avoid.",
    "Correct. DataSync transfers data between storage in other clouds and AWS storage services, makes an initial copy and then scheduled incremental transfers until cutover, and includes encryption and integrity validation. Transfers between Amazon S3 and object storage in other clouds do not need a DataSync agent, so there is nothing to host.",
    "An SFTP endpoint depends on people uploading files by hand. It does not copy an existing bucket and has no incremental sync or verification of the source against the destination.",
    "A file gateway presents an S3 bucket to on-premises applications as an NFS or SMB share. It is not a tool for copying data from another provider's object storage."],
 ref:[], doc:"datasync"},

{id:"q15-021", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Migration & transfer"],
 q:"A payments company is replacing its on-premises SFTP server with AWS Transfer Family, with the files stored in Amazon S3. About 300 banks connect to the server, and each bank's firewall allows outbound SFTP only to IP addresses that it has listed for the company. Changing the lists takes the banks months, so the addresses must be fixed and must stay the same for the life of the service. Which configuration meets these requirements?",
 o:["A server with the public endpoint type, with the banks told to list the published AWS IP address ranges.",
    "A server with the public endpoint type behind an Amazon Route 53 alias record with a long TTL.",
    "A server with a VPC-hosted internal endpoint that the banks reach through an interface VPC endpoint.",
    "A server with a VPC-hosted, internet-facing endpoint that has Elastic IP addresses attached."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Fixed IP addresses are not supported on the public endpoint type. The banks would have to list the whole published AWS address ranges and follow every change to them, which is not a fixed set of addresses.",
    "A DNS record gives a stable name, not stable addresses. The addresses behind a public endpoint are not fixed, whatever the TTL of the record.",
    "An internal endpoint is reachable only from inside the VPC and networks connected to it. The banks connect over the internet and have no interface endpoint into the company's VPC.",
    "Correct. With a VPC-hosted endpoint and the internet-facing option, Elastic IP addresses are attached directly to the endpoint and become its addresses, which AWS names as the way to give clients fixed IPs to allowlist."],
 ref:[], doc:"transfer"},

{id:"q15-022", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Migration & transfer","EFS"],
 q:"A company runs a Linux SFTP server for 600 employees who sign in with their corporate Active Directory user names and passwords. Applications on Amazon EC2 instances in three Availability Zones must read the uploaded files through a shared POSIX file system. The company wants to retire the server and use a managed service, and the employees must keep signing in with their Active Directory credentials. Which combination of choices for an AWS Transfer Family server meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Use the service managed identity provider and import an SSH public key for each employee.",
    "Use Amazon EFS as the storage for the server and mount the file system on the EC2 instances.",
    "Use AWS IAM Identity Center as the identity provider for the SFTP server endpoint.",
    "Use Microsoft Active Directory through AWS Directory Service as the identity provider.",
    "Use an Amazon EBS volume with Multi-Attach as the storage for the server."],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["Service managed users authenticate with SSH keys that are stored in the service. The employees would have to stop using their Active Directory passwords and all receive keys.",
    "Correct. Transfer Family transfers files into and out of Amazon S3 and Amazon EFS. An EFS file system can be mounted over NFS by the instances in all three Availability Zones, which gives the applications a shared POSIX file system.",
    "The identity provider options for server endpoints are service managed, Microsoft Active Directory and a custom identity provider. IAM Identity Center is what Transfer Family web apps use.",
    "Correct. With Active Directory as the identity provider, access is granted to AD groups and their members authenticate with their AD credentials over SFTP.",
    "Transfer Family stores data in Amazon S3 or Amazon EFS. An EBS volume is not one of its storage options."],
 ref:[], doc:"transfer"},

{id:"q15-023", day:15, dom:4, tags:["Migration & transfer","Hybrid connectivity"],
 q:"A research institute that is new to AWS must move 600 TB of instrument data from NAS arrays in its data center to Amazon S3 within eight weeks. The arrays stay in production during the move. The only link to the outside is a shared 200 Mbps internet connection, on which the transfer would take most of a year. A network provider that is an AWS Direct Connect Delivery Partner already serves the building and can deliver a multi-gigabit connection within two weeks. The institute has never ordered AWS Snow Family devices. Which approach should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Order AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices, copy the data to them and ship them back to AWS.",
    "Run AWS DataSync over the existing internet connection with a bandwidth limit during working hours.",
    "Run AWS DataSync over a temporary AWS Direct Connect hosted connection obtained from a Direct Connect Delivery Partner.",
    "Create an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection over the internet link and copy the data with the AWS CLI."],
 a:[2],
 x:["AWS Snowball Edge is no longer available to new customers, and AWS ends support for Snowball devices in commercial Regions on December 31, 2026. The exam guide still lists the AWS Snow Family, so on the exam expect it as the answer for very large offline transfers over a slow link.",
    "DataSync is the right tool, but the internet link is the bottleneck: at 200 Mbps, 600 TB does not fit into eight weeks whatever tool sends it.",
    "Correct. For new customers AWS recommends DataSync for online transfers and, where bandwidth is the limit, DataSync with a Direct Connect hosted connection procured from a Delivery Partner for the duration of the project. For data on portable storage devices AWS offers Data Transfer Terminal instead, which is not this case.",
    "A VPN adds encryption on the same 200 Mbps link and removes no bottleneck. The CLI also lacks the scheduling and verification that DataSync provides."],
 ref:[], doc:"snowball-change"},

{id:"q15-024", day:15, dom:3, tags:["Regions & AZs"],
 q:"A company runs a real-time collaborative design application for architecture firms. Most of its new customers are in one large city that is far from the nearest AWS Region but has an AWS Local Zone, and the round trip to that Region makes the application feel slow. The interactive tier needs single-digit-millisecond latency to those users, who connect over office fiber, home broadband and several mobile carriers. The company has no data center in the city and does not want to install or host any hardware. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Order an AWS Outposts rack and place it in a colocation facility in the city.",
    "Run the interactive tier in a subnet in the AWS Local Zone in that city, extending the existing VPC.",
    "Run the interactive tier in an AWS Wavelength Zone of a carrier that operates in the city.",
    "Keep the tier in the Region and add an Amazon CloudFront distribution with edge caching in front of it."],
 a:[1],
 x:["An Outpost is AWS capacity installed at a site that the customer provides and that must meet facility, power and network requirements. The company wants no hardware and no site to look after.",
    "Correct. Local Zones place compute, storage and other resources close to large population centers for single-digit-millisecond latency, without the customer operating a data center. After the zone is enabled, a subnet created in it extends the Region's VPC, and the zone has its own connection to the internet for users on any network.",
    "A Wavelength Zone sits at the edge of one communications service provider's network and serves the devices on that network. These users arrive over fiber, broadband and several different carriers.",
    "CloudFront shortens the path for content it can cache and optimizes the connection, but every interactive request still travels to the distant Region, so the distance remains."],
 ref:[], doc:"local-zones"},

{id:"q15-025", day:15, dom:3, tags:["Regions & AZs"],
 q:"A company is building a driver-assistance service for delivery vans. Each van sends camera frames over a mobile carrier's 5G network and must receive a hazard warning within a few milliseconds. Tests show that most of the delay arises after the traffic leaves the carrier's network and crosses the internet to the nearest AWS Region. The inference servers run on Amazon EC2, and the rest of the application stays in the Region. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Run the inference servers in a subnet in an AWS Wavelength Zone of the carrier, behind a carrier gateway.",
    "Run the inference servers in the Region and put AWS Global Accelerator in front of them.",
    "Run the inference servers on an AWS Outposts rack that is installed in the company's main depot.",
    "Run the inference servers in the Region and serve the responses through Amazon CloudFront."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. Wavelength deploys AWS compute and storage at the edge of a communications service provider's network for low latency to mobile devices. A VPC is extended to the Wavelength Zone, a carrier gateway lets in traffic from the carrier network, and the instances there communicate with the rest of the application in the parent Region.",
    "Global Accelerator moves traffic onto the AWS network at a nearby edge location, but the requests still travel all the way to the Region. The latency budget is spent on that distance.",
    "An Outpost brings AWS capacity to one customer site. The vans are on the road and reach the service through the carrier's network, not through the depot's local network.",
    "CloudFront helps with cacheable content and connection handling. Every camera frame is a new request that must be computed by the inference servers in the Region."],
 ref:[], doc:"wavelength"},

{id:"q15-026", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Regions & AZs"],
 q:"A hospital group must keep medical images and the systems that process them inside its own data center, because a regulator requires this patient data to remain on premises. The imaging application also controls scanners on the local network and needs very low latency to them. The engineering team wants to use Amazon EC2, Amazon EBS and Amazon RDS with the same APIs and tools as in its AWS Region, and it does not want to buy or maintain server hardware itself. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Run the workload in the AWS Local Zone that is closest to the data center.",
    "Deploy AWS Storage Gateway in the data center and run the processing in the Region.",
    "Run the workload in an AWS Wavelength Zone of the hospital's mobile carrier.",
    "Install an AWS Outposts rack in the hospital's data center."],
 a:[3],
 x:["A Local Zone is an AWS site near a population center. The data would be held in AWS facilities outside the hospital's data center, which the regulator does not allow.",
    "Storage Gateway gives on-premises applications access to storage that lives in AWS. The images would be stored and processed in the Region, not on premises.",
    "A Wavelength Zone is located in a carrier's network to serve mobile devices. It is neither inside the hospital's data center nor on the scanners' local network.",
    "Correct. Outposts is a fully managed service that extends AWS infrastructure, services, APIs and tools to customer premises, with hardware that AWS owns and manages. EC2 instances, EBS volumes and RDS instances run in Outpost subnets on site, which suits low-latency access to local equipment and data that must remain on premises."],
 ref:[], doc:"outposts"},

{id:"q15-027", day:15, dom:3, tags:["Global Accelerator"],
 q:"A company operates a voice chat service that uses a custom protocol over UDP. All of its servers run behind a Network Load Balancer in one AWS Region, and the company cannot deploy to more Regions this year. Users in Asia and South America report choppy audio, and measurements show high jitter on the public internet between them and the Region. The company wants to improve the connection quality for these users without changing the application. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Put an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the Network Load Balancer.",
    "Create Amazon Route 53 latency-based routing records that point to the Network Load Balancer.",
    "Create an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator with the load balancer as its endpoint.",
    "Enable cross-zone load balancing on the Network Load Balancer."],
 a:[2],
 x:["CloudFront delivers content over HTTP and WebSocket. A custom protocol over UDP cannot pass through a distribution.",
    "Latency-based routing picks the lowest-latency Region among the Regions that have records. With one Region there is nothing to choose between, and DNS does not change the network path the packets take.",
    "Correct. Global Accelerator supports TCP and UDP and fits non-HTTP uses such as voice over IP. Users' traffic enters the AWS global network at the edge location closest to them and travels over it to the endpoint, which lowers latency and jitter compared with the public internet, also for an application in a single Region.",
    "Cross-zone load balancing spreads requests evenly across targets in all enabled Availability Zones. It changes nothing on the path between distant users and the Region."],
 ref:["d10c1"], doc:"ga"},

{id:"q15-028", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Global Accelerator","Load balancing"],
 q:"A software company serves an HTTPS API from Application Load Balancers in eu-west-1 and plans to add us-east-1 next year. Its enterprise customers allow outbound traffic only to IP addresses on an allowlist, and their change process permits at most two IPv4 addresses per vendor. The addresses must stay the same when the second Region is added and if the load balancers are ever replaced. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Put a Network Load Balancer with one Elastic IP address per Availability Zone in front of each Region's Application Load Balancer.",
    "Create an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the load balancers and attach an Anycast Static IP list for allowlisting.",
    "Create Amazon Route 53 alias records for the Application Load Balancers and give customers the record's current addresses.",
    "Create an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator and add each Region's Application Load Balancer as an endpoint."],
 a:[3],
 x:["A Network Load Balancer does give one static address per Availability Zone, but an Elastic IP address is for use in one Region only. The second Region would bring a second set of addresses and break the limit of two.",
    "CloudFront Anycast Static IPs do give a fixed list, but for the allowlisting use case the list holds 21 IPv4 addresses, far more than the two the customers accept.",
    "An alias record returns the load balancer's addresses, which the service assigns and can change. Customers would be listing addresses that are not guaranteed to last.",
    "Correct. Global Accelerator provides two static anycast IPv4 addresses that stay assigned for as long as the accelerator exists. Application Load Balancers in one or more Regions sit behind them as endpoints, so Regions and load balancers can be added or replaced without any change on the customers' side."],
 ref:["d10c1"], doc:"ga"},

{id:"q15-029", day:15, dom:2, tags:["Global Accelerator","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A company runs a TCP-based trading gateway behind Network Load Balancers in us-east-1 and us-west-2. Clients connect to the two static IP addresses of an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator. Because the order book is kept only in us-east-1, all clients, including those closer to us-west-2, must use us-east-1 during normal operation. If the us-east-1 endpoint becomes unhealthy, traffic must move to us-west-2 automatically. How should the accelerator be configured?",
 o:["Add both Regions as endpoint groups and set the endpoint weight of the us-west-2 load balancer to 0.",
    "Add both Regions as endpoint groups and set the traffic dial of the us-west-2 endpoint group to 0.",
    "Add only us-east-1 as an endpoint group and add us-west-2 by script when an alarm fires.",
    "Add both Regions as endpoint groups and turn on client affinity for the listener."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Failover goes to a healthy endpoint with a weight greater than zero. An endpoint with weight 0 is excluded: when us-east-1 fails, Global Accelerator finds no healthy endpoint with a weight above zero and ends up routing to a random endpoint in the closest endpoint group, which is not a controlled failover.",
    "Correct. A traffic dial of 0 stops normal traffic to an endpoint group, so clients near us-west-2 are served by us-east-1. For failover Global Accelerator ignores the traffic dial: when us-east-1 has no healthy endpoint, it fails over to a healthy endpoint in another endpoint group, including one whose dial is set to 0.",
    "The standby would depend on custom automation working at the worst moment, which is not the built-in failover the accelerator already offers through health checks.",
    "Client affinity keeps one client's requests on the same endpoint. With both groups active, clients near us-west-2 would still be routed to us-west-2 in normal operation."],
 ref:[], doc:"ga"},

{id:"q15-030", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Hybrid connectivity","IAM Identity Center & federation"],
 q:"A consulting company has 400 employees who work from home and from client sites on company laptops. They need encrypted access to internal applications in private subnets of a VPC. Users must sign in through the company's SAML 2.0 identity provider, and only members of the finance group may reach the subnet that hosts the finance application. The company does not want to deploy or patch VPN servers. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Create an AWS Client VPN endpoint that uses SAML-based federated authentication and associate it with subnets of the VPC.",
    "Create an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection with a customer gateway for each employee's home router.",
    "Add authorization rules that allow each network only for the matching identity provider group.",
    "Order an AWS Direct Connect connection and create a private virtual interface to the VPC.",
    "Launch a bastion host in a public subnet and give every employee an SSH key for it."],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["Correct. Client VPN is a managed, client-based VPN service that scales with the number of users and lets them reach AWS resources from any location with an OpenVPN-based client. It supports single sign-on through SAML-based federated authentication.",
    "Site-to-Site VPN connects a network, such as an office, through a customer gateway device. It is not built for hundreds of individual laptops that move between home and client networks.",
    "Correct. An authorization rule restricts which users can reach a network: for a given network it names the identity provider group that is allowed, so only the finance group reaches the finance subnet.",
    "Direct Connect is a dedicated connection from a fixed location such as a data center. It cannot serve people who work from changing places.",
    "A bastion is a server the company must run and patch, SSH keys bypass the identity provider, and SSH tunnels are not access to web applications by group."],
 ref:[], doc:"client-vpn"},

{id:"q15-031", day:15, dom:2, tags:["Hybrid connectivity","High availability design"],
 q:"A company connects its data center to a VPC over one AWS Direct Connect connection with a private virtual interface to the VPC's virtual private gateway. Routes are exchanged with BGP. The company has decided to add an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection over the internet as a backup. Traffic must use Direct Connect whenever it is available and must move to the VPN automatically, without anyone changing routes, when Direct Connect fails. How should the VPN connection be set up?",
 o:["On a second virtual private gateway, with a Route 53 failover record that points to the VPN.",
    "On the same virtual private gateway with static routes that are more specific than the Direct Connect prefixes.",
    "On the same virtual private gateway with BGP, advertising the same prefixes as over Direct Connect.",
    "On the same virtual private gateway, with a Lambda function that swaps the VPC route tables on an alarm."],
 a:[2],
 x:["The Direct Connect routes and the VPN routes must meet on the same gateway for it to choose between them, and DNS records do not decide which network path packets take between a data center and a VPC.",
    "Routing uses the longest prefix match first. More specific routes on the VPN would attract the traffic to the VPN all the time, also while Direct Connect is healthy.",
    "Correct. When the prefixes are the same, a virtual private gateway prefers BGP routes from Direct Connect over routes from a Site-to-Site VPN connection. With BGP on both paths, the Direct Connect routes disappear when that connection fails, and VPC traffic fails over to the VPN automatically.",
    "This replaces a routing protocol with custom code that must detect the failure and rewrite routes correctly. The gateway already makes this choice by itself when both paths use BGP."],
 ref:[], doc:"vpn"},

{id:"q15-032", day:15, dom:1, tags:["Hybrid connectivity","Encryption in transit"],
 q:"A bank reaches its VPCs through a 1 Gbps AWS Direct Connect hosted connection, a transit virtual interface, a Direct Connect gateway and a transit gateway. A new policy requires IPsec encryption between the bank's router and AWS for all traffic on this path. The security team forbids public IP addresses on the VPN endpoints and does not allow a public virtual interface, and the bank does not want to run VPN appliances in its VPCs. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable MACsec on the Direct Connect connection with a pre-shared key pair.",
    "Create a public virtual interface and a Site-to-Site VPN connection to the VPN service's public endpoints.",
    "Rely on the encryption that AWS applies between Direct Connect locations and Regions.",
    "Create a private IP Site-to-Site VPN connection to the transit gateway over the transit virtual interface."],
 a:[3],
 x:["MACsec is layer 2 encryption on the link between the customer's router and the Direct Connect location, not IPsec, and it is available on dedicated connections, not on hosted connections like this one.",
    "This gives IPsec over Direct Connect, but the tunnel endpoints have public IP addresses and the design needs a public virtual interface. The security team forbids both.",
    "Direct Connect does not encrypt traffic in transit by default. AWS does encrypt data at the physical layer between Direct Connect locations and Regions, but that is not IPsec and does not cover the link from the bank's router.",
    "Correct. A private IP VPN is an IPsec Site-to-Site VPN over a Direct Connect transit virtual interface that terminates on the transit gateway and uses private IP addresses, with no public virtual interface and no third-party VPN equipment."],
 ref:[], doc:"vpn"},

{id:"q15-033", day:15, dom:1, tags:["AWS Backup"],
 q:"A software company stores its backups in an AWS Backup vault. After an operator deleted recovery points by mistake, management wants the vault protected so that requests to delete a recovery point or to shorten its retention are denied for every user while the protection is in place. No regulation applies, and the company expects its retention needs to change, so a small security team with dedicated IAM permissions must remain able to remove the protection itself later. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in governance mode to the vault.",
    "Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode to the vault with a grace time of three days.",
    "Copy every recovery point to a second vault in the same account and Region.",
    "Enable continuous backups with point-in-time recovery in the backup plan."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. In a locked vault AWS Backup denies any attempt, including by the root user, to delete a backup or change its lifecycle. A vault locked in governance mode can have the lock removed by users with sufficient IAM permissions, which keeps the door open for the security team.",
    "In compliance mode the lock can be changed or removed only during the grace time. After the three days the vault and its lock are immutable and cannot be changed by any user or by AWS, so the security team could never remove it.",
    "A second vault that is not locked can be emptied by the same mistake or the same person. A copy is not protection against deletion.",
    "Continuous backups allow restores to a point in time. They do not stop anyone from deleting recovery points or changing their retention."],
 ref:[], doc:"backup"},

{id:"q15-034", day:15, dom:2, tags:["AWS Backup","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A company protects its Amazon EFS file systems, Amazon DynamoDB tables and Amazon Aurora clusters with one AWS Backup plan in eu-central-1. An audit produced two findings. First, all recovery points are in the same Region as the workloads. Second, nobody has ever restored one, so there is no proof that the backups work or how long a restore takes. The company wants to close both findings with the LEAST operational overhead. Which combination of actions should it take? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Add a copy action to the backup rule that copies each recovery point to a vault in another Region.",
    "Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode to the existing vault.",
    "Create an AWS Backup Audit Manager framework and schedule a daily report.",
    "Create a restore testing plan in AWS Backup that restores selected recovery points on a schedule.",
    "Ask each team to restore one backup by hand every quarter and record the result in a ticket."],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Correct. AWS Backup can copy backups to other Regions automatically as part of a scheduled backup plan, which puts a copy of every recovery point away from the workloads.",
    "Vault Lock stops recovery points from being deleted or having their lifecycle changed. It creates no copy in another Region and proves nothing about restores.",
    "Backup Audit Manager evaluates backup activity against controls and produces reports. It can report on the findings, but it does not copy recovery points or perform a restore.",
    "Correct. Restore testing runs automated, periodic restores to evaluate whether recovery points can be restored and records how long the restore jobs take; the results serve as evidence for auditors.",
    "Manual restores would give some proof, but they depend on every team remembering to do them, which is more ongoing work than a testing plan that runs by itself."],
 ref:[], doc:"backup"},

{id:"q15-035", day:15, dom:2, tags:["AWS Backup","Organizations & SCPs"],
 q:"A company deploys a daily backup plan to all 30 of its accounts with a backup policy in AWS Organizations. The policy selects resources by a list of ARNs that the backup team maintains for each account. Teams create new Amazon DynamoDB tables and Amazon EFS file systems every week, and an incident showed that several of them had never been backed up. The company wants every resource that a team marks as production to be protected without further action by the backup team. Which change meets this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Create an AWS Config rule that reports resources that have no recovery points.",
    "Invoke a Lambda function on resource-creation events to add each new ARN to the policy.",
    "Turn on each service's own backup feature, such as point-in-time recovery, in every account.",
    "Change the policy to select resources by a tag, and have teams apply that tag to production resources."],
 a:[3],
 x:["A rule that reports unprotected resources finds the gap after the fact. Someone still has to add each resource to the policy.",
    "This automates the list, but the company would own a function, event rules in 30 accounts and policy updates that can fail. That is more to operate than a selection rule in the policy itself.",
    "Service-level features are configured per resource and per service, outside the central plan. Retention, schedules and reporting would no longer be managed in one place.",
    "Correct. A backup plan can assign resources by tags instead of by ARN. Every supported resource that carries the tag is picked up by the plan in its account, so a new table or file system is protected as soon as its team tags it."],
 ref:[], doc:"backup"},

{id:"q15-036", day:15, dom:3, tags:["Elastic Beanstalk"],
 q:"A team of four developers maintains a Java web application that is packaged as a WAR file and runs on Tomcat. Today it runs on EC2 instances that the team configures by hand. The team wants AWS to provision the load balancer, the Auto Scaling group and health monitoring from the uploaded application, and to apply new platform patch versions in a weekly maintenance window without reducing capacity. The team has no container experience, wants to keep SSH access to the instances, and does not want to pay a fee for the service itself. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Package the application as a container image and run it as an Amazon ECS service on AWS Fargate.",
    "Create a launch template and an Auto Scaling group, and patch the instances with Systems Manager Patch Manager.",
    "Deploy the application to an AWS Elastic Beanstalk environment with managed platform updates enabled.",
    "Rewrite the application as AWS Lambda functions behind Amazon API Gateway."],
 a:[2],
 x:["This requires the team to containerize the application and learn task definitions and services, and on Fargate there are no instances to connect to with SSH.",
    "This can work, but the team itself assembles and maintains the load balancer, scaling, health checks, deployments and patch schedule. That is the manual work it wants AWS to take over.",
    "Correct. Elastic Beanstalk provisions and manages the environment from the uploaded application and leaves the EC2 instances accessible. Managed platform updates apply new platform versions in a scheduled maintenance window while the application stays in service with no reduction in capacity, and there is no additional charge for Elastic Beanstalk beyond the resources used.",
    "A rewrite into functions is a redesign of the application, not a way to host the existing WAR file, and there are no instances to access."],
 ref:[], doc:"beanstalk"},

{id:"q15-037", day:15, dom:2, tags:["Elastic Beanstalk"],
 q:"A company runs a customer-facing web application in a load-balanced AWS Elastic Beanstalk environment. For each release the team wants to send 10% of incoming client traffic to the new version for 15 minutes while the remaining users stay on the current version. If the new instances stay healthy, all traffic should move to them; if not, traffic should return to the old instances automatically. The team does not want to create and manage a second environment. Which deployment policy meets these requirements?",
 o:["Traffic splitting",
    "Immutable",
    "Rolling with additional batch",
    "All at once"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. A traffic-splitting deployment launches a full set of new instances, forwards a specified percentage of incoming client traffic to the new version for a specified evaluation period, then moves all traffic if the new instances stay healthy or moves it back and terminates them if they do not. It is how Elastic Beanstalk does canary testing inside one environment.",
    "An immutable deployment also launches a full set of new instances in a separate Auto Scaling group and removes them if they fail health checks. It has no setting for sending a chosen share of client traffic to the new version for an evaluation period.",
    "This policy keeps full capacity by launching an extra batch first and then updates the instances batch by batch. Old and new versions serve requests side by side, but not in a controlled 10% share with automatic return.",
    "All at once deploys the new version to every instance simultaneously, and all instances are out of service for a short time. No user stays on the old version."],
 ref:[], doc:"beanstalk"},

{id:"q15-038", day:15, dom:4, tags:["Data transfer costs","Internet & NAT gateways"],
 q:"A company runs application servers in private subnets in three Availability Zones. All three subnets route internet-bound traffic to a single NAT gateway in the first Availability Zone. The servers send several terabytes per day to a third-party payment provider's API on the internet. The bill shows a large charge for data transfer between Availability Zones, caused by the servers in the other two zones sending traffic to the NAT gateway. The servers must stay in private subnets in all three zones. Which change reduces this charge?",
 o:["Create gateway VPC endpoints and add them to the route tables of the private subnets.",
    "Replace the NAT gateway with an internet gateway route in the private subnets' route tables.",
    "Attach a second Elastic IP address to the existing NAT gateway.",
    "Create a NAT gateway in each Availability Zone and route each private subnet to the NAT gateway in its own zone."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Gateway endpoints exist for Amazon S3 and DynamoDB. The traffic here goes to a third party's API on the internet, which no VPC endpoint of the company can reach.",
    "A route to an internet gateway would make the subnets public, and instances without public addresses could not use it. The servers must stay private.",
    "A second address raises the number of simultaneous connections the NAT gateway supports. The traffic from the other two zones would still cross Availability Zones to reach it.",
    "Correct. AWS advises that when resources send a significant volume of traffic across Availability Zones to a NAT gateway, they should be in the same zone as the NAT gateway, or a NAT gateway should be created in each zone with resources. Traffic then stays inside its zone, and each zone also keeps internet access if another zone fails."],
 ref:["d11c3"], doc:"nat"},

{id:"q15-039", day:15, dom:4, tags:["Data transfer costs","S3 features"],
 q:"A genomics company stores sequencing output in an Amazon S3 bucket in eu-west-1. A fleet of Amazon EC2 instances in eu-central-1 reads every new object once, about 8 TB per day in total, and writes small result files back to the bucket. The company chose eu-central-1 only because an engineer was familiar with it; no rule ties the compute to that Region. Data transfer between the two Regions has become one of the company's largest charges. Which change removes this charge?",
 o:["Run the processing instances in eu-west-1, the same Region as the bucket.",
    "Create a gateway VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 in the eu-central-1 VPC.",
    "Replicate the bucket to eu-central-1 with S3 Cross-Region Replication and read the replica.",
    "Peer a VPC in eu-west-1 with the eu-central-1 VPC and read the bucket through the peering connection."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. Data transferred from an S3 bucket to AWS services within the same Region as the bucket is not charged, while access to services in a different Region incurs a charge for data transfer across Regions. Moving the compute next to the data removes the inter-Region transfer.",
    "AWS recommends gateway endpoints for access to S3 from a VPC in the same Region as the bucket. An endpoint in eu-central-1 does not make a bucket in eu-west-1 local, and the bytes would still cross Regions.",
    "With Cross-Region Replication the company pays for inter-Region data transfer out from S3 to the destination Region, plus the replication requests and the second copy's storage. Every object is read once, so the same volume would still cross Regions.",
    "Standard inter-Region data transfer charges apply to traffic across an inter-Region peering connection. The data would cross Regions exactly as before."],
 ref:["d11c3"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q15-040", day:15, dom:4, tags:["Data transfer costs","CloudFront"],
 q:"A SaaS company serves a single-page web application through Amazon CloudFront from an S3 origin. Viewers are spread over every continent and all of them must keep the current performance. The cache hit ratio is already above 95%, yet CloudFront data transfer out is the largest item on the bill. Most of the bytes are large, uncompressed JavaScript, CSS and JSON files, and viewers' browsers send the <code>Accept-Encoding</code> header. Which change reduces the data transfer charge?",
 o:["Enable Origin Shield in the Region that is closest to the bucket.",
    "Turn on automatic object compression in the distribution's cache behaviors.",
    "Change the distribution to a price class that uses fewer edge locations.",
    "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the origin bucket."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Origin Shield adds a caching layer that reduces the requests that reach the origin. With a hit ratio above 95% the origin is hardly used, and the bytes sent to viewers stay the same.",
    "Correct. CloudFront can compress objects when the viewer's request includes the <code>Accept-Encoding</code> header. The cost of CloudFront data transfer is based on the total amount of data served, so serving compressed text files costs less, and the smaller files also download faster.",
    "A smaller price class stops serving viewers from the more expensive edge locations, which sends those viewers to more distant ones. The company requires that all viewers keep the current performance.",
    "Transfer Acceleration speeds up transfers into and out of a bucket's accelerated endpoint over long distances. It changes nothing about the bytes that CloudFront sends to viewers."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudfront"}
);
